# Inactive local-date intervals — author freeze 1

Implemented and locally tested; **independent review pending**. This is an additive prerequisite, with no caller or UI activation. There is no publication, deployment, SDK release, account behavior, storage behavior or external adoption in this delivery.

The exact base is site #427 `804c70309d2508e67e8462df526b5f9e71a112e9`. `source-freeze1-identity.json` identifies the three new files and patch. `scope-verification.json` compares every one of the base archive's 10,087 regular files byte for byte: no existing file changed. The scratch uses the existing dependency installation by symlink; Vitest's cache is redirected into this evidence directory. No dependency was installed or added.

## Callable contract and algorithm

`resolveLocalDateIntervals(date, timeZone, provider?)` returns immutable integer-millisecond half-open intervals with `existing`, an explicit `empty` array, or `unresolved` with a bounded reason and no partial intervals. Dates must be Gregorian astronomical years 0000–9999. Numeric internal arithmetic permits the exclusive end in 10000, without accepting expanded public date syntax. It does not apply a birth-time gap/fold policy.

Let `w` be the requested date's nominal UTC midnight and `D = 86,400,000`. Membership is exactly `w <= t + offset(t) < w + D`. For offsets strictly between `-D` and `D`, every member lies inside `[w-D, w+2D)`. A complete sequence of offset transitions divides that finite window into constant-offset segments. Intersect each segment with `[w-offset, w+D-offset)`, discard empty intersections, and merge only touching intervals. This preserves disconnected dates, empty dates, and a repeated continuous 48-hour date; a single enclosing range would lose membership information.

The provider marker names a **trusted semantic obligation**, not evidence that arbitrary code is complete. `nextTransitionMilliseconds` must return the first offset change strictly after the argument, including correct post-transition offsets, with stable synchronous methods. Sampling is explicitly inadmissible. Runtime validation detects malformed/nonadvancing/sub-millisecond boundaries, inconsistent observed offsets and excess work. It cannot detect every deliberately omitted pair of changes that returns to the same offset. Nor can a synchronous caller preempt a provider that never returns. The cap is 32 interior transitions, with at most 33 next-transition calls; exhaustion returns `unresolved`, never a partial interval set.

The optional default adapter feature-detects the host's native Temporal transition interface only when called. It loads no polyfill. It also validates the existing Intl timezone-identifier boundary and checks native Temporal offsets against the existing Intl offset reader. Host mismatch or failure yields unresolved. Import performs no Temporal lookup, Intl formatting, network or storage work. No independent tzdb is introduced, and no stronger historical-data guarantee than the host supplies is claimed.

The authoritative Temporal contract provides strictly advancing transition enumeration and the offset bound used here. See [the transition operation](https://tc39.es/proposal-temporal/#sec-temporal-getnamedtimezonenexttransition) and [the offset operation](https://tc39.es/proposal-temporal/#sec-temporal-getoffsetnanosecondsfor), consulted during this task on 2026-09-08. Two calls to [start-of-day](https://tc39.es/proposal-temporal/#sec-temporal-getstartofday) would not enumerate a disconnected date. An hourly offset scan was rejected because finite counterexamples in the preceding architecture review contain hidden changes. A polyfill or separately shipped tzdb was outside this authorized slice. Native capability absence remains explicit until a reviewed consumer compatibility policy is selected.

## Executed verification

- Node 22.23.2 / Vitest 3.2.6: 79 focused tests passed in the final run. They cover exact lower/upper window and date/transition boundaries; first and last milliseconds; seconds; 0000/0099/9999; Toronto, St Johns, Apia; complete synthetic hidden-change schedules; invalid/missing/failing providers; inconsistent offsets on either side; duplicate and sub-millisecond transitions; 32/33 transition exhaustion; immutable results; missing native capability and native/Intl disagreement; and import laziness.
- Strict TypeScript check passed. The initial check caught a test-only direct cast of deliberately malformed providers (TS2352). It was corrected to an explicit `unknown` intermediate cast. Both the failure log and successful rerun are retained. Initial 79-unit-test success is also retained. No product failure was observed in the executed author tests.
- Actual Chrome 152.0.7977.83: 22 explicit native historical/date fixtures and four signed offset identifiers passed. The 22 fixtures include empty and repeated Apia dates, disconnected St Johns and Goose Bay dates, Toronto's cross-midnight gap, Manaus's four-second gap, Mexico City seconds, ordinary and half-hour DST, and years 0000/0099/9999. All 100 first/last and adjacent boundary membership checks agreed with independent Gregorian Intl date formatting. Four signed offset forms were accepted by both native interfaces and returned the exact expected intervals.
- The native run also checked missing capability, invalid broader Temporal input and provider failure without empty/partial results. Immediately after import all monitored effects were zero; after execution all monitored storage and network API effects remained zero. Only the owned document and bundle were requested. The disposable browser and loopback server were closed.
- The patch passed reverse applicability validation against the exact frozen source.

These are finite tests on the recorded runtime, not a claim of all-runtime native availability or all historical tzdb correctness. The initial 17 native fixture expectations were retained from the prior endpoint architecture review, whose reference discovery was bounded and independently confirmed with native transition enumeration. Five additional explicit controls were added here. Full application build, visual regression and release gates are unnecessary to claim author verification of this inactive slice; they are not claimed as executed for it.

`native-freeze1/result.json` contains every input, exact expected/actual interval, boundary observation, runtime, source hash, request, effect count and cleanup. Its esbuild source map inputs include the unchanged date parser, locale constants and existing offset reader. `commands.json` records the actual verification invocations. The independent reviewer receives the exact source freeze and uses a separate scratch; author testing is not represented as independent approval.
